abstract: >-
  Efficient autonomous exploration requires robots to select informative
  destinations while adapting their decisions to an evolving map.
  Short-sighted frontier selection and continued navigation toward
  targets whose information value has diminished can result in
  unnecessary travel. We present frontier_exploration_ros2, an integrated
  framework for mobile robot exploration that couples frontier sequence
  planning with visibility-aware goal execution. The framework refines
  occupancy grids before wavefront frontier extraction and evaluates
  candidate transitions using a Minimum Ratio Traveling Salesman
  Problem (MRTSP)-based cost model. A bounded-horizon dynamic programming
  solver searches frontier sequences within a pruned candidate set,
  executing only the first selected target before replanning as the
  map evolves. During navigation, occlusion-aware ray casting estimates
  the remaining unknown-space visibility from the target pose, allowing
  goals with insufficient expected reveal to be preempted. Costmap
  constraints and optional failure-memory-based frontier suppression
  address blocked and repeatedly unsuccessful targets. Incremental
  decision-map updates and reusable caches reduce redundant computation
  throughout the exploration cycle. Simulation benchmarks in bookstore
  and warehouse environments evaluate exploration time, travel distance,
  and computational resource use against existing exploration packages.
  In the reported warehouse comparison, the dynamic programming
  configuration reduces travel distance by 22.1% and exploration time
  by 24.7% relative to m_explore_ros2. The framework is released as
  open-source software with a reusable C++ core and ROS 2 integration
  to support reproducible evaluation and further research.

keywords:
  - autonomous exploration
  - mobile robotics
  - frontier-based exploration
  - occupancy grid maps
  - decision-map optimization
  - wavefront frontier detection
  - minimum ratio traveling salesman problem
  - bounded-horizon dynamic programming
  - candidate pruning
  - ray casting
  - visibility-aware preemption
  - frontier suppression
  - incremental map processing
  - ROS 2
